/-!
# Principality of reflexive ideals and faithfully flat local descent

Source: EGA IV4, proof of Theorem 21.14.1, first paragraph, with EGA IV 2.5.2;
Stacks Project, Tags 0EB9, 0AVB and 00DV
(blueprint: lem-sfb-reflexive-ideal-principal-flat-descent).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

namespace Ideal

/-- A nonzero reflexive ideal becomes principal when its height-one prime divisors
upstairs are principal, and principality descends along the faithfully flat local map.
The height-one condition is expressed exactly as in the node: the prime is nonzero
and has no prime strictly between zero and itself. Locality is the equality of the
inverse image of the upstairs maximal ideal with the downstairs maximal ideal.

Source: EGA IV4, proof of Theorem 21.14.1, first paragraph (divisorial extension and
product of powers of principal prime divisors), EGA IV 2.5.2 (local descent);
Stacks Project, Tags 0EB9, 0AVB and 00DV
(blueprint: lem-sfb-reflexive-ideal-principal-flat-descent).

Proof plan, following the six steps of the node:
1. Set J = I.map f. The ideal I is finite by
   `lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-submodule-finite`. Faithful flatness gives flatness;
   `lem-weil-pbc-flat-tensor-exact` identifies the injective multiplication map from
   the scalar extension of I into S with J. Thus J is finite and reflexive by
   `lem-per-flat-base-change-reflexive-modules`. It is nonzero, since faithful
   flatness reflects the vanishing of the scalar extension of I.
2. Choose a nonzero b in J. By `lem-trf-normal-domain-principal-divisor-kernel`,
   height-one localizations of S are DVRs and the valuations of b have finite
   support. Every height-one prime containing J gives a nonunit b in its local
   ring, hence a nonzero valuation. Therefore those primes form a finite list
   p_1, ..., p_t. The hypothesis supplies nonzero generators x_i of p_i.
3. By `def-sc-discrete-valuation-ring` and
   `lem-weil-pbc-dvr-unit-times-uniformizer-power`, the localization of J at p_i
   is generated by x_i raised to a nonnegative integer n_i; x_i is a uniformizer
   and the localized ideal is nonzero.
4. Put c = product of x_i ^ n_i, using c = 1 for the empty list. It is nonzero.
   Distinct height-one primes are incomparable. At p_i all factors except x_i
   are units, so cS and J have the same localization. At any other height-one
   prime J contains a unit and every x_i is a unit, so both localizations are
   the whole local ring. These assertions use the fraction description in
   `def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime`.
5. Both J and cS are finite reflexive submodules of the fraction field: for cS
   use its identification with S by multiplication by c. Their localizations
   agree at all height-one primes, so
   `lem-per-reflexive-height-one-intersection-criterion` gives J = cS. This
   proves the first conclusion, including J = S when the prime list is empty.
6. Locality induces the residue-field inclusion k = R/m into l = S/n.
   `lem-weil-pbc-tensor-over-quotient-ring` and the identification of step 1 give
   (I/mI) tensor_k l isomorphic to J/nJ: the middle tensor-associativity map
   sends i tensor lambda to (i tensor 1) tensor lambda, with inverse
   (i tensor s) tensor lambda mapping to i tensor (s mod n) lambda.
   Since J = cS, J/nJ has at most one generator. Apply
   `lem-weil-pbc-residue-extension-minimal-generators` with M = I and r = 1.
   It gives one generator for I, proving the second conclusion, also for I = R. -/
theorem isPrincipal_map_and_self_of_isReflexive
    {R : Type u} {S : Type v} [CommRing R] [CommRing S]
    [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsNoetherianRing S] [IsDomain R] [IsDomain S]
    [IsIntegrallyClosed R] [IsIntegrallyClosed S] [IsLocalRing R] [IsLocalRing S]
    (f : R →+* S) (hfaithful : f.FaithfullyFlat)
    (hlocal : Ideal.comap f (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal S) = IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R)
    (I : Ideal R) (hI : I ≠ ⊥) [Module.IsReflexive R I]
    (hprime : ∀ p : Ideal S, p.IsPrime → p ≠ ⊥ →
      (∀ q : Ideal S, q.IsPrime → ⊥ < q → q < p → False) →
      I.map f ≤ p → p.IsPrincipal) :
    (I.map f).IsPrincipal ∧ I.IsPrincipal := by
  sorry

end Ideal
